# velero bootstrap

Velero owns the **cloud** half of the backup story only. The local
(external drive) half is `helm/backup-local` — a restic CronJob. See
`docs/adr/0005-backup-restore.md` for why they are two different tools.

## 1. Cloud bucket/region — done

`helm/velero/values.yaml` `backupStorageLocation[0]` (name: `cloud`) points
at `loki-homelab-backup` / `ap-south-1`, and the `velero` namespace +
`velero-cloud-credentials` Secret (key `cloud`, AWS-credentials-file blob)
already exist in-cluster — reused the access key from an older host-cron
tar+gpg+S3 backup job, created directly via `kubectl` (never written to a
file that outlived the command, never committed).

**This Secret is now covered by `scripts/k8s-secrets/copy_secrets.sh`.** It
previously was not: that script only scanned `homelab-*` namespaces, so the
credential needed to *read the backups* existed nowhere but the cluster the
backups exist to replace. Re-run the export after any key rotation.

### The IAM policy needs widening — Velero needs more than the old cron job did

That key came from a host cron job that only ever uploaded a tarball, so its
policy grants writes but not `s3:ListBucket`. Velero validates a
BackupStorageLocation by listing it, so the BSL comes up `Unavailable`:

```
BackupStorageLocation "cloud" is unavailable: ... api error AccessDenied:
User: arn:aws:iam::<acct>:user/loki-homelab-backup-service is not authorized
to perform: s3:ListBucket on resource: "arn:aws:s3:::loki-homelab-backup"
```

The policy in place scopes everything to an `automatic/` prefix — both the
`s3:ListBucket` condition and the object ARNs. `values.yaml` therefore sets
`prefix: automatic` on the BSL to match; the two must stay in sync, and
changing one without the other puts the location back into `Unavailable`.

If a large backup later fails part-way through an upload, add
`s3:ListMultipartUploadParts` on `.../automatic/*` and
`s3:ListBucketMultipartUploads` on the bucket — Velero's reference policy
includes them and the 75G immich library will exercise multipart uploads.

Reference policy (no EC2 permissions needed — this cluster uses Kopia
file-system backup, not volume snapshots):

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "s3:GetObject",
        "s3:PutObject",
        "s3:DeleteObject",
        "s3:AbortMultipartUpload",
        "s3:ListMultipartUploadParts"
      ],
      "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::loki-homelab-backup/*"]
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::loki-homelab-backup"]
    }
  ]
}
```

Then confirm it flips to `Available` (revalidation is on a 1-minute loop):

```
kubectl -n velero get backupstoragelocation
```

## 2. The CRD job is disabled — don't re-enable it

The chart's CRD install/upgrade Job is why **Velero never installed at all**:
no CRDs, no server, no node-agent, no Schedule, no backups, ever. It defaults
to `docker.io/bitnami/kubectl` with a tag derived from the cluster version,
and Bitnami retired their public Docker Hub catalog, so the image 404s.

Two replacement images were tried and both failed differently:
`registry.k8s.io/kubectl` is distroless, and the job's init container runs
``/bin/sh -c 'cp `which sh` /tmp && cp `which kubectl` /tmp'`` — no shell, so
`Init:RunContainerError`. `alpine/kubectl` has a shell, but stages a
musl-linked busybox that the velero image cannot exec (`exec /tmp/sh: no such
file or directory`). The staged binaries have to match velero's libc.

So `values.yaml` sets `upgradeCRDs: false` and the CRDs come from the chart's
own `crds/` directory, which Helm and ArgoCD apply directly — no third-party
image in the backup system's critical path.

**Trade-off:** Helm never upgrades `crds/`. On a velero chart bump, check the
release notes and apply CRD changes by hand:

```
kubectl apply -f https://github.com/vmware-tanzu/velero/releases/download/<ver>/velero-crds.yaml
```

Verify the install actually completed:

```
kubectl -n velero get pods                    # velero + node-agent Running
kubectl get crd | grep velero                 # CRDs present
kubectl -n velero get schedules.velero.io     # cloud-weekly listed
```

## 3. Install the velero CLI (for manual backup/restore commands)

```
# https://velero.io/docs/latest/basic-install/#install-the-cli
```

## The jobs

**Cloud backup — automatic.** The `cloud-weekly` Schedule runs `0 3 * * 0`
(Sundays 03:00) against the `cloud` BSL, `includedNamespaces: ["*"]`, 30-day
TTL. Trigger one out of band:

```
velero backup create cloud-manual-$(date +%Y%m%d) --storage-location cloud-manual
velero backup describe cloud-manual-$(date +%Y%m%d) --details
```

**Cloud restore — manual, always.**

```
velero backup get
velero restore create --from-backup <backup-name>
velero restore describe <restore-name>
```

**Local backup and restore** — see `helm/backup-local/.setup`. Not Velero.

## Verify a restore actually works

An untested restore is the usual way "I had backups" becomes data loss. Do
this once now and after any major change:

```
velero restore create verify-$(date +%Y%m%d) \
  --from-backup <backup-name> \
  --include-namespaces homelab-atuin \
  --namespace-mappings homelab-atuin:restore-test
kubectl -n restore-test get pods,pvc
# confirm the atuin Postgres actually starts and has data, then:
kubectl delete namespace restore-test
```

`homelab-atuin` is the cheapest realistic test — small, and it exercises the
Postgres dump-hook path that immich/yggdrasil depend on.

## Scope note

`arr`'s media/torrents tree is a hostPath mount, not a PVC (see
`helm/arr/values.yaml`) — fs-backup only captures PVC-backed pod volumes, so
it is excluded automatically. That exclusion is deliberate; ADR 0005 records
why.
